## Problem

The player's clan colors do **not** persist on world-map party icons (the icons that render when you see other parties next to your own on the campaign map â€” armies, garrisons, lord parties). The icons fall back to vanilla TaleWorlds clan colors instead of the user-customized colors set via the banner editor.

This is contrary to the documented behavior of the BannerColorPersistence feature, which CLAUDE.md describes as "UI color persistence + 3D battle + conversation â€” player clan colors **everywhere**." World-map party icons are part of "everywhere".

Symptom is silent â€” there's no crash, no log, no warning. The Postfix just returns immediately on every call.

## Analysis

`Main/Features/BannerColorPersistence/Hooks/MobilePartyVisual_AddCharacterToPartyIcon_Patch.cs` declares:

```csharp
private static IBannerColorService? _service;
private static IBannerHeroAdapter? _heroAdapter;

public static void Initialize(IBannerColorService service, IBannerHeroAdapter heroAdapter)
{
    _service = service;
    _heroAdapter = heroAdapter;
}

public static void Postfix(CharacterObject characterObject, ref uint teamColor1, ref uint teamColor2)
{
    var info = _heroAdapter?.GetClanColorInfo(characterObject);
    if (info == null) return;
    if (!(_service?.ShouldUseClanColor(info.Value) ?? false)) return;

    teamColor1 = info.Value.Color1;
    teamColor2 = info.Value.Color2;
}
```

The Postfix uses `_service` and `_heroAdapter` static fields. Both are populated by `Initialize(...)`. Without an `Initialize` call, both remain null. The null-conditional operators (`?.`) silently fail-open: `GetClanColorInfo` returns null â†’ `info == null` â†’ Postfix returns without modifying `teamColor1` or `teamColor2`.

The Harmony Postfix **is** wired at `Main/SubModule.cs:447`:

```csharp
var mobilePartyTarget = MobilePartyVisual_AddCharacterToPartyIcon_Patch.TargetMethod();
if (mobilePartyTarget != null)
    _harmony.Patch(mobilePartyTarget, postfix: new HarmonyMethod(
        typeof(MobilePartyVisual_AddCharacterToPartyIcon_Patch),
        nameof(MobilePartyVisual_AddCharacterToPartyIcon_Patch.Postfix)));
```

So Harmony attaches the Postfix correctly. Every call to `MobilePartyVisual.AddCharacterToPartyIcon` invokes our Postfix â€” which then returns immediately because the statics are null.

Looking at SubModule.cs lines 161-180, **19 other BannerColorPersistence patch classes are explicitly initialized** in sequence:

```csharp
Banner_TryGetBannerDataFromCode_Transpiler.Initialize(bannerColorConfig, logger);
Clan_UpdateBannerColorsAccordingToKingdom_Patch.Initialize(bannerColorService);
// ... 17 more patches ...
OrderOfBattleHeroItemVM_RefreshInformation_Patch.Initialize(bannerColorService, bannerHeroAdapter);
```

`MobilePartyVisual_AddCharacterToPartyIcon_Patch.Initialize` is missing from this list. Grep confirms zero callers anywhere in the codebase:

```
$ grep -rn "MobilePartyVisual_AddCharacterToPartyIcon_Patch.Initialize" Main TAOM.Tests
(no matches)
```

Likely root cause: when the patch class was added, the author forgot to add the corresponding `.Initialize(...)` line to the `OnSubModuleLoad` initialization block. The patch compiles, the Harmony binding compiles, and there's no test that exercises the world-map party-icon code path with mocks for the service â€” so the miss survived to ship.

This is functionally similar to the Messengers crash (#121): a feature is wired in some places but not others, and the omission survives because there's no compile-time link between "patch class exists" and "patch is correctly initialized." The difference is severity: Messengers crashed (high signal), MobilePartyVisual silently no-ops (low signal â€” a player would only notice if they directly compared their icon colors before and after this feature was introduced).

Identified during Phase 1 of the TAOM feature audit (`docs/audits/wiring-matrix.md`).

## Solution (sketch â€” fix lands in Phase 9, not this session)

Single-line addition in `Main/SubModule.cs::OnSubModuleLoad`, sorted alphabetically with the existing block (between line 167 `SandBoxUIHelper_GetCharacterCode_Patch.Initialize(...)` and line 168 `SPInventoryVM_UpdateCurrentCharacterIfPossible_Patch.Initialize(...)` based on type-name order â€” or in any locally-coherent position in the BannerColorPersistence block):

```csharp
MobilePartyVisual_AddCharacterToPartyIcon_Patch.Initialize(bannerColorService, bannerHeroAdapter);
```

Verification:
- Build green.
- In-game: visit campaign map with player clan color set to a non-vanilla pair (e.g., bright purple). Confirm the player's own party icon and nearby army icons render in the chosen clan color (not the vanilla fallback).
- Confirm the same scene previously showed vanilla colors before the fix.

## Files changed (one-line fix)

- `Main/SubModule.cs` â€” +1 line in `OnSubModuleLoad` BannerColorPersistence initialization block.

## Testing

- `./build.ps1` â€” verify no compile error (the new `Initialize` call already type-checks per the patch's signature).
- Manual: world-map party-icon color check as above.

## Phase 9 reminder

Per Phase 1's no-fix constraint, this issue is filed but NOT fixed. Phase 9 batches wiring fixes. When picked up, this one is mechanical â€” sub-5-min fix + manual smoke-test.

## Finding (P2 â€” performance)

`AiMilitaryBehavior_CalculateDistanceScoreForBesieging_Patch` Postfix calls `IoC.Resolve<IArmyTargetingService>()`, `IoC.Resolve<IArmyTargetingSettingsProvider>()`, and `IoC.Resolve<IModLogger>()` on every invocation with no static caching. Per the feature doc, the patched method fires per-army per-AI-tick (~500-2000 calls/cycle). Violates `.claude/rules/harmony-patches.md`: "Before \ in hot path, use lazy cache." The early exit `if (bestDistanceScore > 0f) return;` reduces but doesn't eliminate impact â€” armies targeting zero-score besiegeable settlements (the case the patch is for) call all 3 resolves.

## Fix sketch

```csharp
private static IArmyTargetingService? _service;
private static IArmyTargetingSettingsProvider? _settings;
private static IModLogger? _logger;

[HarmonyPostfix]
public static void Postfix(...) {
    if (bestDistanceScore > 0f) return;
    _service ??= IoC.Resolve<IArmyTargetingService>();
    _settings ??= IoC.Resolve<IArmyTargetingSettingsProvider>();
    _logger ??= IoC.Resolve<IModLogger>();
    // ... existing logic
}
```

